---
title: Basecamp — run your fleet from one place — FrontierJS
description: Basecamp is the control plane for FrontierJS apps — ship a release to a box and roll it back, run it under an agent that owns TLS and replication, keep keys and secrets in one trust model, watch it, and restore it.
render: static
---
<!--
  The Basecamp page: the product, argued in five sections. Prerendered, no
  script beyond the layout's theme swatches.

  ── Section contract ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Each section is a component in components/basecamp/, and its root is
  `<section id=… class="bc-section">` holding a `.container.stack`:

    p.eyebrow                       the topic's name, as the nav says it
    h2                              the claim, in the reader's words
    p.lede                          one or two sentences, one idea
    .bc-split
      ul.bc-points > li             <strong>head.</strong> one sentence each, 3–4
      figure.bc-proof               ONE proof: a pre.code of a real command or
                                    output, or a .flow of hops, with a figcaption
                                    saying where it came from
    p.bc-more                       "Read more:" links into the repo docs

  The structure rules are components/ProductFrame.mesa's, so five sections
  cannot drift into five looks. A section adds a scoped rule only for what its
  proof needs.

  Every claim and every terminal line is lifted from the package docs or code
  and is something that runs TODAY. Direction is labeled as direction. A
  section that cannot back a claim drops the claim.
-->
<script>
  import ProductFrame from '../components/ProductFrame.mesa'
  import Deploy  from '../components/basecamp/Deploy.mesa'
  import Outpost from '../components/basecamp/Outpost.mesa'
  import Trust   from '../components/basecamp/Trust.mesa'
  import Watch   from '../components/basecamp/Watch.mesa'
  import Backups from '../components/basecamp/Backups.mesa'
</script>

<ProductFrame>

<section id="top" class="bc-hero">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Basecamp · alpha</p>
    <h1>Your servers,<br>one command center.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Basecamp is where you go when you stop building one app and start operating
      several. Ship a release, roll it back, see what is running where, and get your
      data back when something breaks — on boxes you own, with no one in the middle.
    </p>
    <div class="cluster" style="gap:.75rem">
      <a class="btn primary raised" href="/start/basecamp/">Put Basecamp on a box</a>
      <a class="btn outlined" href="#deploy">See how it works</a>
    </div>
    <nav class="bc-jump" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#deploy">Deploy</a>
      <a href="#outpost">Outpost</a>
      <a href="#trust">Trust</a>
      <a href="#watch">Watch</a>
      <a href="#backups">Backups</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</section>

<Deploy />
<Outpost />
<Trust />
<Watch />
<Backups />

<section id="start" class="bc-section">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Start</p>
    <h2>One command buys the box.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Name a hostname. <code>fli basecamp:create</code> buys the server, installs Basecamp,
      points DNS at it and makes your first admin.
    </p>
    <div class="cluster" style="gap:.75rem">
      <a class="btn primary raised" href="/start/basecamp/">The quickstart</a>
      <a class="btn outlined" href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/tree/main/packages/basecamp">Read the source</a>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">
      Basecamp is alpha and built in FrontierJS itself. It is a face over a documented
      path: everything it does is also available from <code>fli</code> and, beneath that, ssh.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

</ProductFrame>

